{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Cancer is a complex, multilevel phenomenon [1-5] in which health outcomes depend on highly interrelated elements across stages of etiology, prevention, screening, diagnosis, treatment, survivorship, and end-of-life care [6, 7]. Standard bench and clinical research approaches alone are not well suited to account for this complexity, as most cancer control interventions often focus on explicit, often linear health outcomes of a specific, well-defined population [1, 8, 9]. ... Although system dynamics modeling has been utilized in various aspects of public health since the 1970s, past reviews in this field have noted a significant increase in their use since the early 2000s, especially in areas focused on chronic or non-communicable diseases [24, 25]. The extent to which system dynamics modeling has been applied specifically to cancer prevention and control has not been studied.",
      "reason": "The introduction describes the current state of knowledge about cancer complexity and system dynamics modeling, notes increased use over time, and explicitly states the gap/uncertainty that application to cancer prevention and control has not been studied."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "In a 2019 report, the National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine argued that systems science methods could help illustrate the multilevel nature of cancer effects and costs to society and help cancer control efforts be more agile and adaptive [7]. The Journal of the National Cancer Institute published several reports calling for the use of complex, multilevel, or systems-focused approaches to advance the landscape of cancer prevention and control [1, 2, 10]. Systems models, particularly well calibrated simulation models, provide a relatively lowcost and low-risk opportunity for comparing potential interventions or treatments in silico before involving busy clinics and patients in trials [1, 7, 11, 12]. ... The extent to which system dynamics modeling has been applied specifically to cancer prevention and control has not been studied.",
      "reason": "The authors cite authoritative calls for systems approaches and argue system dynamics modeling can address needs in cancer control, and then state the lack of study of its application to cancer — explaining the importance and need for this review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Although system dynamics modeling has been utilized in various aspects of public health since the 1970s, past reviews in this field have noted a significant increase in their use since the early 2000s, especially in areas focused on chronic or non-communicable diseases [24, 25]. The extent to which system dynamics modeling has been applied specifically to cancer prevention and control has not been studied.",
      "reason": "The manuscript states that the extent of application to cancer prevention and control has not been studied, indicating no prior similar systematic review exists to update or cite, so this conditional item does not apply."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_5",
      "quote": "This review was conducted according to PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analysis) 2020 guidelines [29] (S1 Appendix). Scholarly articles describing the development or application of system dynamics models for cancer prevention and control were identified, abstracted, and analyzed. ... A formal research protocol was not created for this review. The study was not registered in the International Prospective Register of Systematic Reviews (PROSPERO) as it was a methods review and did not assess health outcomes.",
      "reason": "The review is a methods review of system dynamics modeling and did not assess intervention effects or health outcomes, so describing mechanisms of action for interventions is not applicable."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_5",
      "quote": "The study was not registered in the International Prospective Register of Systematic Reviews (PROSPERO) as it was a methods review and did not assess health outcomes.",
      "reason": "The review is a methods review rather than of complex interventions or delivery contexts; therefore inclusion of a logic model to show hypothesised relationships between intervention components and outcomes is not applicable."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 88
}

